---
import MadeByKaj from '../components/MadeByKaj.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Layout title="About | Found in Space" description="Found in Space makes scientific data and models into materials people can explore, question and build with. Our educational ambition is data and model literacy.">
	<SiteHeader section="About" />
	<main class="page page--narrow">
		<header class="hero hub-hero">
			<p class="eyebrow">About Found in Space</p><h1>Materials to think and create with.</h1>
			<p class="lede">We make scientific data and models into materials people can explore,
				question, and build with—helping them understand how evidence becomes understanding,
				and pursue questions of their own.</p>
		</header>
		<article class="prose">
			<h2>Explore. Question. Build.</h2>
			<p>Scientific material becomes tangible through star fields, worlds, eclipse predictions,
				maps, and musical experiments. A representation gives us something to notice, play with,
				and ask questions about. Astronomy is a strong starting point; other measured systems
				and scientific models belong here too.</p>
			<p>Exploring, questioning, and building are equally valid ways to participate. You can enjoy
				an experience, investigate the evidence, or make something of your own. There is no required
				sequence, and taking part does not require learning to code.</p>
			<h2>The experience opens onto its materials.</h2>
			<p>Behind a view are sources, measurements, transformations, assumptions, and decisions about
				what to show. Our aim is to make those understandable at a useful depth, and make the
				materials practical to reuse for another investigation, visualisation, lesson, game, or artwork.</p>
			<p>A measured quantity, an inferred distance, an eclipse prediction, and an artistic mapping
				are different kinds of material. Keeping those differences visible helps people judge what
				a representation can tell them—and where a different measurement or model is needed.</p>
			<h2>Data and model literacy is the educational ambition.</h2>
			<p>We want to help people recognise which questions the available material can answer,
				investigate them, and judge what the evidence supports. Curiosity, play, and making can
				generate a worthwhile question, just as a structured investigation can help pursue one.</p>
			<p>The <a href={`${basePath}teach/`}>teaching collection</a> offers astronomy activities and
				adaptable prompts. Suggested levels and timings are starting points for educators’ judgement.
				Claims about learning outcomes, use with younger children, and classroom effectiveness
				require evaluation; they are ambitions to work towards.</p>
			<h2>Why this matters as answers become easier to obtain.</h2>
			<p>AI and other analytical tools can help people work with data. People still need enough
				understanding to decide what matters, recognise what is answerable, and judge a result.
				Those skills are useful whether an answer comes from a person, a model, or an automated tool.</p>
			<h2>Open materials, with a clear account of availability.</h2>
			<p>Public <a href="https://github.com/Found-in-Space">Found in Space repositories</a> include
				data pipelines, catalogue publications, models, viewer components, and authoring tools.
				Each project has its own setup, release status, and licence terms. Aeolian Measure is
				currently in private development; its source and player are not publicly linked.</p>
			<p>The <a href={`${basePath}build/`}>Build route</a> helps you find useful materials. The
				<a href="https://github.com/Found-in-Space/.github/blob/main/docs/projects.md">developer project map</a>
				explains the technical ecosystem and contribution routes.</p>
			<h2>Related experiments</h2>
			<p>Separately maintained projects share this approach:
				<a href="https://github.com/kws/galaxy-js">Galaxy-JS</a> explores simplified gravitational
				rules through galactic encounters, and <a href="https://github.com/kws/cern-map-overlay">CERN map overlays</a>
				connect scientific dimensions to familiar places. They are related work, with their own repositories.</p>
			<h2>Who is behind it?</h2>
			<p>Found in Space is built by <a href="https://www.k-si.com/">Kaj Siebert</a>, who trained as
				an astrophysicist before working in data science and technology. The project brings together
				science, data, code, creative work, and an interest in how people learn.</p>
			<p>Kaj’s science outreach with CERN through <a href="https://www.thebigbangcollective.com/">The Big Bang Collective</a>
				shares the same spirit: make room for play, curiosity, and the pleasure of finding something out.</p>
		</article>
		<nav class="page-nav" aria-label="Find a starting point"><a href={`${basePath}explore/`}>Find an experience →</a><a href={`${basePath}build/`}>Find materials to build with →</a></nav>
		<MadeByKaj />
	</main>
</Layout>
